.TH SKYDSEC 1 "2026-08-08" "Skydio" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
skydsec \- query a Skydio security compliance daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B skydsec
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.I address
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B skydsec
connects to a
.BR skydsecd (8)
daemon at the given IPv4
.I address
on TCP port 2014, writes everything the daemon sends to standard output,
and exits.
.PP
The client sends no request; connecting is the entire protocol.  Output
is whatever the daemon reports, so the field order is defined by
.BR skydsecd (8)
rather than by this program.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Report the address and port being contacted on standard error before
connecting.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I address
The IPv4 address of the daemon, in dotted-quad notation.  Host names are
not accepted.  Exactly one address must be given; options may appear
before or after it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The daemon was contacted and its reply written to standard output.
.TP
.B 1
No address was given, the address was not valid IPv4, the connection
failed, or an error occurred while reading the reply or writing to
standard output.
.PP
Diagnostics are written to standard error, so the reply on standard
output can be redirected or piped without them being interleaved.
.SH EXAMPLES
Query a daemon running on the local host:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ skydsec 127.0.0.1
myhost.example.com
/J5B5GK3/CNCMK002670720/
ws1HubUtil installed
Sat Aug  8 00:33:49 2026
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR skydsecd (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Alvin Cura <alvin.cura@skydio.com>
